1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,850
“What do you think I should do?” asks your friend, as she sits beside you, waiting for your advice.
“你觉得我该怎么办？”你的朋友坐在你身边问道，等着你给她出主意。

2
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:09,410
This is no easy situation.
这种情况可一点也不好应对。

3
00:00:10,210 --> 00:00:15,710
Say something your friend disagrees with or something unhelpful, and you might feel you’ve disappointed her.
要是你说了朋友不认同的话，或者说了些没有帮助的话，你可能会觉得自己让她失望了。

4
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:21,000
It’s human nature to want to offer solutions when someone comes to you with a problem.
有人带着难题来找你时，你会想给对方提供解决办法，这是人的天性。

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,270
It’s also easy to fall into the trap of simply saying “Everything will be OK”.
我们也很容易落入一个陷阱：只是说一句“一切都会好起来的”。

6
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:29,410
This is not actually useful for your friend.
这样说其实对你的朋友并没有用。

7
00:00:30,210 --> 00:00:33,650
But we often say that because we believe we need to say something.
但我们常常这么说，是因为我们认为自己需要说点什么。

8
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:40,640
This is because we’re glad that our friend has come to us for help, even if we don’t really think we’re able to give it.
这是因为我们很高兴朋友来向我们求助，哪怕我们其实并不觉得自己帮得上忙。

9
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:44,210
So is it a good idea to try to give advice at all?
那么，试着提建议到底是不是个好主意呢？

10
00:00:45,010 --> 00:00:47,680
The answer is yes… if it’s done in the right way.
答案是肯定的……如果方式得当的话。

11
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:52,400
In fact, friends often really only want someone to listen to them.
事实上，朋友往往真的只是想有人听他们说说话。

12
00:00:53,200 --> 00:01:00,760
It’s important to try to work out whether they’re actually asking for advice, or simply wanting to talk over whatever is going round in their head.
重要的是要设法弄清楚，他们是真的在征求建议，还是只想把脑子里转来转去的事情都拿出来聊一聊。

13
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,120
Talking may make people feel better all by itself.
光是说说话，本身就可能让人感觉好一些。

14
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:08,210
There’s no harm in asking whether they’d like you to give suggestions.
问问他们想不想让你给些建议，也没什么坏处。

15
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:10,830
It’s also important to be honest.
诚实也很重要。

16
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:15,870
If you’re unable to think of a way to solve your friends’ problem, just say so.
如果你想不出办法解决朋友的问题，那就直说。

17
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:22,030
Your friends won’t feel upset, and you can still listen carefully and try to put yourself in their shoes.
朋友不会难过，而且你仍然可以认真倾听，试着设身处地为他们着想。

18
00:01:22,830 --> 00:01:25,790
Then you can go away and try to think of something helpful.
然后你可以先离开，试着想出一些有帮助的办法。

19
00:01:26,590 --> 00:01:30,760
You can ask yourself, “What would I do if I were to experience the same thing?”
你可以问问自己：“假如我遇到同样的事情，我会怎么做？”

20
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:35,210
Later you can go back to your friends with a few helpful suggestions.
之后，你可以带着几条有帮助的建议，再去找朋友。

21
00:01:36,010 --> 00:01:38,640
Sometimes a problem doesn’t go away overnight.
有时候，问题不会一夜之间就消失。

22
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:45,280
It can be hard to listen to your friends going over and over the same thing without being able to help them.
听朋友翻来覆去地说同一件事，自己却帮不上忙，这可能会很难熬。

23
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,730
They will certainly be thankful if you just allow them to “talk it out”, though.
不过，如果你只是让他们“把心里话说出来”，他们一定会很感激。

24
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:55,900
Maybe they’re building up courage to take action, and need to convince themselves they’re doing the right thing.
也许他们正在积攒采取行动的勇气，还需要说服自己，让自己相信这样做是对的。

25
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,350
Just being there for them may be all you have to do to make things better.
要让情况好起来，你或许只需要陪在他们身边就够了。
